  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 329 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nDespite the presence of a sprawling ridge of high pressure\nextending from Quebec to the southern Plains, central Illinois\nremains locked in overcast conditions early this morning.\nPersistent boundary layer moisture continues to be trapped\nbeneath a strong subsidence inversion and the E/NE low-level\nflow is doing little to nothing to help scour it out.\nUnfortunately with the overall synoptic pattern changing very\nlittle and surface winds expected to maintain an easterly\ncomponent, prospects for widespread/lasting clearing remain dim\nthrough the upcoming weekend.\n\nAs an upper low evident on 07z/2am water vapor imagery over the\nAppalachians tracks slowly eastward and deepens, it will serve \nas an effective block to any systems crossing the central U.S.\nthrough the weekend. An initial short-wave trough across the\nDakotas will lift northeastward over the next 24-36 hours. The\ntail end of this feature will bring weak synoptic lift into the\nwestern KILX CWA tonight into Saturday. While deep-layer\nmoisture will be limited, most models suggest widely-scattered\nlight showers developing in the Illinois River Valley.\n\nOnce the first wave passes well to the north, a stronger wave\ncurrently over Baja California will approach from the southwest\non Saturday. This feature will weaken as it approaches an upper\nridge locked in place along/east of the Mississippi River:\nhowever, it appears a few showers could spread as far east as\nI-55...especially Saturday night into early Sunday before the\nwave dissipates.\n\n00z Sep 24 synoptic models are in excellent agreement through 96\nhours (00z Mon), then solutions diverge significantly next week.\nAll models show a high-amplitude wave coming onshore along the\nWest Coast on Sunday: however, both the ECMWF and GEM further\namplify the wave into a closed low over the Desert Southwest\nMonday-Wednesday, Meanwhile the GFS and GFS Ensemble maintain a\nmore progressive open wave that quickly traverses the country \nand swings into central Illinois Wednesday into Thursday. \nCurrent teleconnections forecast from both the deterministic \nECMWF and GFS suggest the AO and NAO will become negative early \nnext week, which is supportive of an amplified pattern. Exactly \nhow amplified remains in question at this point. Current NBM \nfeatures rain chances arriving as early as Tuesday. If the \nslower/more amplified ECMWF/GEM verify, precip will likely be \ndelayed until Wednesday...then may linger through the end of the\nweek as the wave only slowly approaches from the southwest. At \nthis point, expect a benign forecast across central Illinois \nthrough the weekend with temperatures climbing well into the \n70s/lower 80s by Monday/Tuesday. After that, things will become \nmore unsettled and thunderstorm chances will return to the \npicture at some point during the Tuesday through Friday time \nperiod.",
